<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gan (Addu Atoll)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gan is the southernmost island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addu-atoll">Addu Atoll</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maldives">Maldives</a>. The British built an airfield there during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and from 1957 to 1976 it was the RAF Gan staging post on routes to the Far East and a main source of local employment. The British presence encouraged the secession of the United Suvadive Republic in 1959, and the airfield is now Gan International Airport.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gan-addu-atoll.jpg" alt="Gan (Addu Atoll)" width="320" height="250" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Addu_Atoll_(Landsat).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Gan is the southernmost island of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maldives">Maldives</a> and, at 2.2561 square kilometres, the second largest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addu-atoll">Addu Atoll</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hithadhoo">Hithadhoo</a>. Its name comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> grama, a village. The island was inhabited from very ancient times, and its people grew yams, manioc and coconuts. Causeways link it to the neighbouring islands of Feydu, Maradu and Hithadhoo. It has a tropical rainforest climate with heavy rain all year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the pre-Islamic era Gan was the head island of Addu and a Buddhist place of learning. In 1922 H. C. P. Bell studied its ruins, the foundations of a vihara and a ruined stupa, the southernmost Buddhist site of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maldives">Maldives</a>; both were bulldozed when the airfield was built, and the islanders were moved to neighbouring islands.</p><p>In 1941 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> chose Addu, with its safe, deep anchorage, for a base known as Port T. Engineers from HMS Guardian cleared airstrips on Gan from August 1941, while Catalina and Sunderland flying boats worked from its sheltered northern shore, and large oil tanks were built. The 1st Royal Marine Coast Defence Regiment manned the batteries, and causeways joined the western islands. The Japanese did not learn of the base until late in the war. In March 1944 the German submarine U-183 torpedoed the tanker British Loyalty through a gap in the nets; she was scuttled in the lagoon on 5 January 1946.</p><p>In 1957 the base passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-air-force">Royal Air Force</a> and served as RAF Gan during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>, until the British withdrew in 1976. Kookaburra rockets were also launched from the island. Many islanders who had worked for the British spoke fluent English, and when the base closed they turned to the new tourist industry; many moved to Malé to seek work in the nearby resorts and schooling for their children.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Gan is relatively large by Maldive standards. After the British left, the airstrip was hardly used for many decades before it was developed into Gan International Airport. The island has a hotel for tourists and a resort, Equator Village.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gan (Addu Atoll)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Abdullah Afeef, a local leader at Addu, where the British had an air base at <b>Gan</b>, was declared president of the United Suvadive Republic, with Hithadhoo as its capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-suvadive-republic">United Suvadive Republic</a></li></ul></section>
